in winds of up to 75 miles per hour  clouds of red sand from the Sahara  Desert is causing chaos in the northern  Canaries leaving hundreds of British  holiday makers stranded on the island  the government has declared a state of  alert as a dust storm which turned the  sky orange has reduced visibility all  flights in and out of Gran Canaria  Airport antenna we've have been  suspended but Marty O'Neill managed to  get off the island on one of the last  flight back to the UK on Saturday it was  quite eerie Lerner looking in places and  I very quickly descended under this red  grip you could feel in your skin in your  teeth you can even feel it in your mouth  and your nose we were one of the last  flights right we met people on the way  home that actually got their flight  turned back and couldn't land the  airports were closing the board was  doing where we were extremely lucky to  get home the Canaries which our popular  tourist destination is now choking in  dust people still on the island  have been advised to stay indoors and  keep their windows and doors shut it's  never been as bad as this for about 10  years or more people who live out here  all the time say it's a very severe  Colima and it's been very very hot an  incredibly dusty so why is this  happening the sandstorms known as the  Kalima regularly affects the holiday  island  it occurs when strong winds bring hot  dry air filled with dust officials are  warning residents to stay off the  streets because of the dust and sand and  as temperatures rise so does the risk of  fires on the island the good news is  though that they do tend to pass quite  quickly so that you know if the  disruption that we're seeing at the  moment with the flights it should pass  and people should be able to get back  from half-turn  a picture-perfect holiday for many has  now turned into a nightmare ordeal and  forecasters fear their worst is yet to  come  no Philips Sky News
 

